00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.760
If I'm gonna inject myself with healing factor, what do I need to look out for here?

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:23.620
Well, after doing the research, it looks like most sources have over-looked the factor's one huge weakness.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:27.180
The fatal-flaw that makes it easy to take down these two characters.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.660
Well 'most' sources have over-looked it anyway

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.320
and it's going to surprise you which one happens to be, the most accurate.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.400
Now first, let me make it clear, that I know both characters have died before.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.480
But honestly, it's tended to result from some pretty cheap means.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:44.700
So I'm discounting all things that turn off or counter-act the healing factor.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.420
That, quite frankly, is a cop-out from writers who wrote themselves into a corner

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.380
by making characters that were too powerful.

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:54.400
In my analysis today, we're finishing these guys off in a perfectly natural way

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.480
that doesn't require some magical anti-heal.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:02.260
Additionally, we're not pulling a Dragon-Ball Z and talking about disintegrating every last cell.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:06.960
Too easy as an explanation, and quite frankly, too difficult to actually execute.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:15.180
I mean, in Uncanny X-men annual #11 Wolverine actually grows his entire body back from a single drop of blood.

