A multi universe Earth crash is the exact reason why Deadpool is currently dead in the comics.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:36.120
But again, I'm just a simple man with a totally average cosmic altering powers,

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.020
and I need something doesn't need to escalate that quickly.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:40.980
Put those nuclear war heads away...

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:41.900
Put 'em away.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:43.000
Nooo.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:43.800
Good. Good.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.940
Because the healing factor's weakness is much simpler than all of this.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:56.560
Wolverine, and especially Deadpool, are vulnerable to every single one of you viewers watching right now and it all has to do with the brain.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.320
Or more specifically, memories.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:03.600
Because, sure these guys may be able to regenerate their whole body from a single drop of blood,

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:07.340
but one thing the factor can't regenerate is memory.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:08.300
Let me explain.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:14.820
You see first thing to understand about memory formation and why you can't regenerate memories is that memories aren't stored.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:16.320
They're not physical things.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:23.040
Neurons, the cells in the brain. Are physical things and healing factor is made to restore or replace damaged cells.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:27.520
But neurons are not the same as memories and that's the problem here.

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:29.700
Memories are signals. A short term memory,

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:35.040
Say the last diet coke you drank, is a pattern of signals in your mind. #NotSponsored.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:36.140
#JustAnAddict

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:41.900
So you drink a diet coke and the sensation you get from it trigger a signal in a specific neurons in your pre-frontal cortex.

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.000
The area that stores short term memory.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:50.140
That signal starts of as an electric potential created by ions moving in and out of the cells at one end of the neuron.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.840
That electrical signal then triggers a chemical signal.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:56.460
Which is a physical transfer of ions from one neuron to another.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:58.760
Physically transferring ions causes the electrical

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:00.700
signals in the next neuron to change.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:02.099
Which is how the electrical and chemical

00:07:02.099 --> 00:07:04.779
signal is carried across a lot of
neurons.

00:07:04.779 --> 00:07:06.720
In short, it's a chain reaction.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.150
So how then, do these signals create
memories?

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:12.930
Well, each of your memories has a
specific chain reaction of signals

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.270
across a specific set of neurons.

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:17.390
Your diet coke signal started the first time

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:22.120
you cracked open a diet coke and felt
it's cool, crisp, refreshing burn slide

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:23.020
down your throat.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:24.500
When you took that first sip,

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:25.940
some of your taste neurons sent

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:30.000
a signal to some of your other taste
neurons in a specific pattern as you thought:

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:30.580
"Mhmm."

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.420
"This is bubbly and habit forming."

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:34.260
The pattern of neurons firing for your

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:38.370
drinking diet coke memory is different
from the pattern for your drinking

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:41.700
orange juice memory or, your tasting
chocolate pattern.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:47.320
It's specifically your diet coke pattern,
and it gives you a specific memory of diet coke.

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:50.390
I-I swear this isn't sponsored. I just have a problem.

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:51.470
Now let me be clear.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:55.750
All electrical signals going on between
two individual neurons is basically the

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:58.150
same across the entirety of your brain.

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:01.800
What makes something a memory is the
specific set of neurons that are

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.080
activated in association with a
particular activity.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:07.720
The brain has a hundred million neurons.

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.780
So if a few of them are involved in each memory.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:12.980
The brain has the theoretical
potential to store almost an infinite

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:16.980
number of memories based on how many
combinations of neurons there are and

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.580
how many unique patterns of signals you can create.

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:23.160
In this way, the brain is an incredible storage system.

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:24.600
And how do these things get stored?

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:26.360
Well, when you experience something once,

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:28.240
a few of your neurons start a signal pattern.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.220
When you think back to remember that experience

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:32.300
your brain is actually recreating

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:34.040
that same signal pattern again.

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:35.970
When you've only experienced something once, you can

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:38.980
only dimly recreate that pattern when you think about it

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:40.630
or it fades away pretty fast.

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:43.430
If you want to improve your recall of that experience

00:08:43.430 --> 00:08:44.980
you have to reinforce it.

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:47.280
To do that, you have to
experience it again.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.020
So you drink a second diet coke.

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:51.020
Then another and before long you have an

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:52.140
emotional dependence.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:53.320
#winning

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:54.930
Every time you repeat the activity you

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:57.270
trigger the same signal pattern over and over again

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:58.230
and your brain starts to

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:00.670
store that pattern in a long-term memory area,

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:01.730
The hippocampus.

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:04.250
The neurons get a little bit closer to each other and, when that

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:07.860
happens, the signal becomes stronger
because chemicals don't have to travel that far.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:10.500
The signals also become stronger and more frequent.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:11.980
So over time those memories become ingrained

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.940
because the signal patterns have been used so much.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:18.320
So then, why can't Wolverine or Deadpool just regenerate memories?

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:22.060
Quite frankly, it's because there's nothing to regenerate.

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:25.200
Based on what we've outlined, the memory isn't part of the neuron,

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:28.100
it's a pattern of signals between neurons.

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:30.270
It's a tricky concept so let me explain it this way.

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:31.379
When you learn about physics

00:09:31.379 --> 00:09:33.079
you learn about electrical currents,

00:09:33.079 --> 00:09:34.859
which is a movement of electrons.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:39.080
You can think of memories in a lot the same way. In physics you can measure an electric

00:09:39.089 --> 00:09:41.289
current, you can turn it on and off, etc.

00:09:41.289 --> 00:09:43.110
But it's not a physical thing you can

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:44.279
hold in your hand.

00:09:44.279 --> 00:09:47.339
The current runs through the wires and generates electricity.

00:09:47.339 --> 00:09:47.850
But just because

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:51.770
you make a wire doesn't mean there is
automatically a current running through it.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:54.960
In humans, you can theoretically rebuild the neurons.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.560
They're a lot like the wires running all through the brain.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.519
But just having the neurons there doesn't mean

00:09:59.519 --> 00:10:02.100
that the right electrical pattern will run through them.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:06.520
Memories only happen when the specific neurons in your brain have already experienced something.

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:09.000
New cells don't come equipped with memories.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.340
When you regenerate an arm, or a leg, or a creepy
little baby hand, those are new body

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:17.300
parts and their function doesn't require you to have experienced something before.

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:20.520
But newly regenerated neurons are like
baby neurons.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:22.820
They're brand new, they're a blank slate!

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:25.340
Which is great because they're intact, but bad because

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:27.970
only neurons with experiences have
memories.

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:30.570
New neurons have to start from scratch,

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:32.560
even if they're all in the right place.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.560
To put it simply, our two super healers

00:10:34.589 --> 00:10:39.089
can grow back the brain cells, sure, but not the brain's electrical patterns.

00:10:39.089 --> 00:10:42.191
And as a result they're a blank shell, a husk.

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:45.380
A being without memories. Think about it! Sure, they may look like

00:10:45.389 --> 00:10:48.349
Wolverine and Deadpool, but they won't
act like them.

00:10:48.349 --> 00:10:53.120
Without those neural connections they lose the elite fighting training and arrogant personalities that

00:10:53.129 --> 00:10:54.660
make them, them.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:58.920
It's something that, in my research,
literally no one has addressed.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:03.200
Well, almost no one, and that is the
biggest irony of all.

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:06.720
In the movie, X-Men Origins: Wolverine,

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:08.360
Yeah, we're talking about that one.

